1 LOAD

   Reads a ROM or NVRAM image, one SAVE wrote, into the virtual VAX's
   memory.  The qualifier that says which must be given.

   Format

     LOAD/ROM [filespec]
     LOAD/NVRAM [filespec]

   A plain LOAD, of a whole machine image, isn't in govax
   (%DCL-E-NEEDROMNVRAM).

2 Parameter

 filespec

   The file.  A VMS file specification (SYS$SYSTEM:DEFAULT.ROM) is a
   file on a mounted volume, a host path a host file, and a bare name a
   file in the default directory when SET DEFAULT is on a volume.  A
   bare name that isn't there, or any name when the default is on the
   host, is looked for in the host's current directory, the --path
   directories, and govax's built-in files.  With /NOERROR it may be
   left out: default.rom or default.nvram.

2 Description

   The image holds its own base address and size, from SAVE.  The ROM
   is console ROM memory, at physical address 20040000 in govax's own
   default.rom; NVRAM is the non-volatile RAM some VAX consoles keep
   their boot data in.  STARTUP.COM loads the system volume's
   SYS$SYSTEM:DEFAULT.ROM.  SHOW ROM and SHOW NVRAM show what is loaded.

2 Qualifiers
/ERROR

      /[NO]ERROR

   /NOERROR makes a file that can't be opened no error: nothing is
   loaded.  A startup procedure can then load an image only if it's
   there.

/NVRAM

   Loads an NVRAM image.

/ROM

   Loads a ROM image.

2 Examples

   1.$ LOAD/ROM SYS$SYSTEM:DEFAULT.ROM

     Loads the system volume's ROM, as STARTUP.COM does.

   2.$ LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR

     Loads default.nvram, if there is one.
